    "body": "Thalidomide enters this private reporting corpus through Wikipedia's “Medical scandals” index. Wikidata's current English description identifies the subject as “immunomodulatory drug known for its ability to cause birth defects.” Those discovery signals are not accepted as verified narrative. The finished article must establish which event, dispute, system or loss the name denotes; where and when it belongs; who created the surviving record; and which primary and independent sources can support a fair chronology.\n\nThis manuscript is a private, AI-assisted archival reconstruction plan. It is not a certified account, an original-reporting claim or a publishable substitute for source review. Its present value is narrower: it identifies one distinct subject, records where that subject was discovered and lays out a concrete path from an index entry to accountable journalism.\n\n## The discovery record\n\nThe orientation trail is unusually explicit. The Wikipedia source is page 51078, captured at revision 1379685705 on 2026-10-11T09:21:45Z. It connects to Wikidata item Q203174, captured at entity revision 2555585209. The Wikidata snapshot supplied the short discovery description “immunomodulatory drug known for its ability to cause birth defects.” Category membership, labels and descriptions can change; none is claim-level proof.\n\nThat boundary matters. An index can establish that a subject is findable, but it cannot establish the event's chronology, the reliability of a number, the meaning of a technical term, the fairness of an allegation or the responsibility of an institution. The reporting pass must open the underlying citations, locate records created closest to the events and preserve uncertainty when the surviving evidence does not converge.\n\n## Build the chronology and mechanism\n\nSeparate clinical observation, surveillance signal, epidemiological association, mechanistic evidence and legal conclusion. For Thalidomide, every date, quantity, quotation and causal statement remains open until the underlying record is located and reconciled.\n\nThe first evidence pass has three defined tasks:\n\n- Locate the earliest contemporaneous record that unambiguously identifies Thalidomide and fixes the scope of the chronology.\n- Obtain the principal investigation, inquiry, court, regulatory or technical record relevant to this public-health record.\n- Find an independent contemporary source and a later retrospective source, then document where either conflicts with the official account.\n\nThe chronology should show conditions before the first documented warning, the decisions made while alternatives still existed, the point at which consequences became difficult to arrest and the evidence created afterward. Eyewitness observation, technical inference, regulatory finding, legal judgment and later interpretation must remain separate. If clocks, names, quantities or causal accounts conflict, the disagreement belongs in the article with its provenance; it must not be silently averaged into a cleaner story.\n\n## Map power, responsibility and affected people\n\nTest the independence, speed and transparency of surveillance, research and risk communication. The reporting must name the institutions specific to Thalidomide, distinguish their powers from their public claims and document meaningful disagreement rather than smoothing it away.\n\nA systems account should widen responsibility without dissolving it. Design, finance, procurement, maintenance, staffing, operations, oversight, emergency response and executive control are different powers. The article must identify which of them actually existed around this subject, who exercised them and what records make that assignment defensible.\n\nThe human-source plan is equally concrete:\n\n- Identify people directly affected by Thalidomide without inferring names, motives or testimony from aggregate accounts.\n- Map the organizations with power to prevent, disclose, investigate, remedy or preserve the record of Thalidomide.\n- Seek a source able to test the dominant explanation, including local, labor, survivor, community or specialist archives where appropriate.\n\nPeople should not appear merely as illustrations of a technical mechanism. Names, health details, losses and testimony require reliable, rights-appropriate sourcing. When records are absent, sealed, translated indirectly or created by institutions with an interest in the outcome, the manuscript should say so rather than fill the gap with narrative confidence.\n\n## Test the public memory\n\nAsk why some health crises become universal reference points while local or marginalized experiences remain difficult to retrieve. The obscurity question for Thalidomide is therefore an evidence problem: the article must show how records were preserved, restricted, dispersed, digitized or repeated before arguing why public memory changed.\n\nObscurity is not evidence of concealment by itself. A subject may recede because records are dispersed, terminology changes, archives remain undigitized, litigation narrows what becomes public, language limits discovery or a more famous comparison supplies an easier story. Each explanation needs evidence. The article should compare contemporary coverage, institutional memory, local commemoration and later summaries before claiming that anything was forgotten.\n\nThe next reporting round should answer three questions:\n\n- What is the earliest claim about Thalidomide that can be tied to a dated primary record?\n- Which widely repeated number or causal statement about Thalidomide has the weakest visible provenance?\n- What changed afterward, who verified implementation and what evidence would show that the change endured?\n\n## Publication boundary\n\nThe two linked pages are discovery sources only. Before publication, an editor must obtain claim-level primary evidence, at least one genuinely independent account, meaningful contrary evidence and a documented reconciliation of dates, identities and quantities. The piece also needs a real named author, a fairness review, rights-cleared media or an explicit text-only decision, and named human editorial approval.\n\nUntil those gates close, this remains private editorial inventory. No reachability check, generated word count, category label, metadata revision or batch test converts it into verified journalism. What is complete now is the reporting map for Thalidomide: Reconstructing the Public-Health Record: a distinct subject, a reproducible discovery trail, explicit evidence tasks and a visible record of every major editorial obligation still open."
